Abstract
Vector databases return opaque scalar similarities over high-dimensional embeddings, giving
practitioners no coordinate-level explanation of a match and no way to restrict HNSW
traversal itself to a dynamic focus subspace. We presentXQdrant, an extension of the
Qdrant engine addressing both problems, and we report results for each honestly rather
than only where they are favorable.
Attributionis unambiguously positive:with dims explainedcomputes exact top-mper-
dimension score decomposition in-database, avoiding client-side vector egress, and is recall-
neutral against vanilla Qdrant while running∼2.2×faster than post-query extraction at
encoder-scale widths (D∈ {768,1536}).
Masked-distance HNSW traversalis a mixed result, and we treat that as the paper’s
second contribution: a systems-level characterization ofwhysubspace-restricted traversal is
harder than it looks. Naive all-layer masking collapses recall (SIFT1M Recall@10 as low
as 0.006 atD sub/D= 0.1) because HNSW’s graph edges encode full-space proximity. A
hybrid design that keeps full-distance routing on coarse layers and masks only near the base
layer (mask from layer) restores navigability (SIFT recall 0.79–0.98 for cutoffs≥1) but
doesnotyield end-to-end latency wins, because graph descent remains dominated by I/O
and candidate-set bookkeeping rather than distance arithmetic; a dedicated SIMD gather
kernel wins microbenchmarks (1.1–1.8×over repack-then-score) without changing this. We
further show that subspace/full-spacek-NNcoherencepredicts when a cheap verification
pass can recover full-space recall, and that anα-weighted blend of full and masked scores is
strictly dominated by a hard layer cutoff.
We release a reproducible harness and an experiment index so each of these positive and
negative findings can be independently reproduced or cited.
Keywords:vector databases, HNSW, approximate nearest neighbor, explainability, feature
attribution, subspace search, masked distance, SIMD
1 Introduction
High-dimensional dense embeddings underpin retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), recom-
mendation, and semantic search [6, 4, 3]. Production engines traverse HNSW graphs [1], col-
lapsing every comparison into a single scalar. This creates two gaps that motivate this paper.
Explainability .Practitioners cannot see which coordinates drove a hit without fetching
full vectors and recomputing per-dimension terms client-side — an expensive, egress-bound
pattern.
Subspace routing.Many workloads care about a dynamic focus setFof coordinates (con-
cept probes, ablations, modality masks), yet stock HNSW always evaluates fullD-dimensional
distances during graph descent. Rescoring onFafter full-vector preselection improves semantic
specificity but, as we show, cannot reduce traversal cost.
