5.4 M2: A Hybrid Cutoff Restores Navigability, Not Latency
M2 keeps full-distance routing on coarse layers, hypothesizing that most of navigability is deter-
mined there. Figure 6 confirms this: on SIFT, cutoffsL≥1 recover 0.79–0.98 recall (evaluated
against subspace-restricted ground truth), whileL=0 tracks the low coherence floor. On high-D
synthetic data the same cutoff lifts otherwise-collapsed cells (e.g.,D=768, ratio 0.25:∼0.01→
∼0.20) but plateaus near 0.15–0.22 — a ceiling we explain via coherence in Section 5.6.
Critically,end-to-end speedup vs. full search remains below 1 in every measured
cell(Figure 8), even using the gather kernel. Section 5.5 isolates why: the arithmetic sav-
ings from masking are real but small relative to the I/O and candidate-management cost that
dominates HNSW wall-clock time.
5.5 Kernel Choice Explains the Ceiling, Not the Floor
Figure 9 isolates the SIMD kernel question directly: gather is∼1.24×/ 1.14×/ 1.42×/
1.77×faster than repack-then-score atD sub ∈ {32,128,384,768}in ns/op microbenchmarks.
Re-running the M2 sweep with gather instead of repack leaves recall unchanged and still pro-
duceszeroend-to-end cells above 1×. This isolates the bottleneck to graph traversal overhead
(candidate heap management, visited-set bookkeeping, memory access to non-contiguous graph
pages) rather than the per-comparison arithmetic — the kernel is not the limiting factor, so
faster kernels alone cannot close the gap identified in Section 5.4.
