Why masked traversal doesn’t yield end-to-end speedup.Even with a faster masked-
comparison kernel, HNSW still touches full vectors for storage layout, graph structure, and
— on hybrid coarse layers — full-precision SIMD. Section 5.5’s isolation experiment shows
I/O and candidate management dominate wall-clock time; the arithmetic XQdrant’s masking
saves is simply not the bottleneck at the scales tested. This is a mechanism-level finding, not an
implementation shortcoming of our specific kernels, and we believe it generalizes to any HNSW-
family engine without a redesigned traversal data structure (e.g., a masked-distance-aware graph
built with a dimension-aware edge structure from the start, rather than an existing full-space
graph reused at query time).
Auxiliary projected indexes (future work).As noted in Section 6, a secondary index
built directly in a registered focus subspace would have its own valid navigability guarantee and
could plausibly deliver the end-to-end speedup that query-time masking of an existing full-space
graph cannot. This is a natural next step but requires an index-registration API and additional
