no change to the underlying graph — but it is not yet a latency win, and subspace/full-space
coherence, not implementation effort, sets a hard ceiling on how much recall a masked traversal
of an unmodified full-space graph can recover. Overfetch-and-verify andα-blending clarify the
remaining design space: the former can recover recall when the data is coherent enough to
make masked candidates useful, at a real latency cost; the latter is strictly dominated by a
hard layer cutoff and should not ship. Together with a public experiment index, these results
give practitioners a concrete, evidence-based answer to “should I mask HNSW traversal by
subspace,” rather than a single untested claim in either direction.
